---
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import productionLine from '../assets/images/production-line.jpg';
---

<BaseLayout
    title="OEM Capping Chuck Compatibility: Zalkin, Krones, AROL, CSI"
    description="What to check before buying aftermarket capping chucks and headsets for Zalkin, Krones, AROL, CSI, and Tedelta cappers. Includes matching chucks to caps like 29/25 and PCO 1881."
    canonical="https://cappingchucks.com/oem-compatibility/"
    ogTitle="OEM Compatibility | CappingChucks.com"
    ogDescription="What to check before buying aftermarket capping chucks and headsets for Zalkin, Krones, AROL, CSI, and Tedelta cappers."
    schemaType="TechArticle"
    breadcrumb="OEM Compatibility"
>
    <PageHero title="OEM Compatibility" crumb="OEM Compatibility" answerLabel="Will it fit?">
        <p>Maybe. The machine brand alone does not tell you. Check the exact machine model, the spindle connection, and the cap before you order an aftermarket chuck or <a href="/capping-headsets/">capping headset</a>.</p>
    </PageHero>

    <main>
        <section id="checklist" class="section">
            <div class="wrap">
                <span class="kicker">Checklist</span>
                <h2>Check these 4 things before you order</h2>
                <ol class="steps prose-col">
                    <li><strong>Machine model and serial number.</strong> Same brand does not mean same parts.</li>
                    <li><strong>Spindle connection.</strong> Keyed, splined, or bayonet, plus the taper and how it locks.</li>
                    <li><strong>The cap.</strong> Size, thread, and tamper band style set the chuck insert shape. See <a href="/cap-sizes/">cap and neck finish sizes</a>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Torque control.</strong> Magnetic, spring clutch, or air, and the control signal it uses.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="platforms" class="section section-alt">
            <div class="wrap">
                <span class="kicker kicker-blue">Brands</span>
                <h2>Capper brands</h2>

                <figure class="mb-8">
                    <Picture
                        src={productionLine}
                        alt="Glass bottles on a rotary capping turret at a wine bottling facility"
                        class="w-full h-56 sm:h-72 object-cover rounded-3xl border-[3px] border-ink"
                        widths={[600, 900, 1200]}
                        sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px"
                        formats={['avif', 'webp']}
                        quality={80}
                        loading="lazy"
                    />
                    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-base text-ink-soft">Rotary capping turret on a glass bottle line. Photo: Pexels</figcaption>
                </figure>

                <div class="cards cards-2">
                    <div class="card card-blue" id="zalkin">
                        <span class="pill">Part of ProMach</span>
                        <h3><a href="https://www.zalkincapping.com/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Zalkin</a></h3>
                        <p>Rotary cappers for drink and food lines. Check the spindle connection, how the machine handles caps, and its torque control.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card card-red" id="krones">
                        <span class="pill">Krones Group</span>
                        <h3><a href="https://www.krones.com/en/products/machines/capper-models-for-a-wide-range-of-applications.php" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Krones AG</a></h3>
                        <p>Capping stations for most kinds of drink lines. Krones and Zalkin are separate companies. Fit depends on the capper setup, how it handles caps, and the change parts on your machine.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card card-yellow" id="arol">
                        <span class="pill">AROL</span>
                        <h3><a href="https://www.arol.com/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">AROL S.p.A.</a></h3>
                        <p>Runs tethered, ROPP, and standard caps. Check the spindle connection, the chuck shape where it meets the cap, and the insert material.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card card-green" id="csi">
                        <span class="pill">Closure Systems International</span>
                        <h3><a href="https://www.csiclosures.com/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">CSI</a></h3>
                        <p>Runs many cap types, including aluminum roll-on caps. Check against the machine you actually have.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card card-blue" id="tedelta">
                        <span class="pill">Tedelta</span>
                        <h3><a href="https://us.tedelta.com/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Tedelta</a></h3>
                        <p>Spanish capper maker. Used on medicine, cosmetics, food, and drink lines. Check the machine serial number, spindle layout, and approved cap.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="match-the-cap" class="section">
            <div class="wrap prose-col">
                <span class="kicker kicker-green">Match the cap</span>
                <h2>Chucks are sized to the cap, not the machine</h2>
                <p>A capper that runs more than one cap needs the right <a href="/">capping chuck</a> or insert for each one. For example, a Krones line might run both 28 mm <a href="/glossary/#pco-1881">PCO 1881</a> soft drink caps and <a href="/glossary/#29-25">29/25</a> still water caps. Those are different neck finishes, so check that the chuck insert matches each cap.</p>
                <p>29/25 means the bottle neck has a 29 mm thread diameter and a 25 mm inside diameter (CETIE standard <a href="https://www.cetie.org/en/finish-data-sheets-gme_125.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">GME 30.26</a>). It is common on still water because it uses less plastic.</p>
                <div class="note">
                    <strong>Trademarks:</strong> Brand names belong to their owners. We use them only to help you identify machines. Confirm fit from machine records, drawings, or a sample part test.
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section section-alt">
            <div class="wrap">
                <h2>Keep reading</h2>
                <ul class="related">
                    <li><a href="/chuck-types/#grip">Chuck body styles</a></li>
                    <li><a href="/capping-headsets/#spring-clutch">Spring-clutch and pneumatic headsets</a></li>
                    <li><a href="/cap-sizes/#sizing-a-chuck">Sizing a chuck to a cap</a></li>
                    <li><a href="/troubleshooting/">Troubleshooting</a></li>
                    <li><a href="/glossary/">Glossary</a></li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>
    </main>
</BaseLayout>
